  <h2>Three ways to get Modbus onto MQTT</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Use it when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a></td><td>You want a headless box at the machine that polls and forwards as Sparkplug B, with no database and no screen. About 15 MB, for amd64, arm64, and arm/v7.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/gateway/">Gateway, Edge, or IoT</a></td><td>You want screens, alarms, and a historian beside the polling. The embedded MQTT broker listens on port 1883.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/flow/">Flow</a></td><td>You want to wire it yourself, with transforms between the register and the topic.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A Modbus TCP gateway for the registers you already have</h2>
  <p>Call it a converter, a bridge, or a gateway: the job is the same. Read the registers, name them, and publish them where the rest of the plant can use them. The <a href="/integrations/modbus/">Modbus TCP gateway driver</a> covers TCP and RTU, and the <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a> can keep the history too.</p>

  <h2>Registers the way the manual prints them</h2>
  <p>A float across two registers is a plausible number even when the word order is wrong, and that is the Modbus mistake that costs people days. HotLoop names every order the vendor manuals print, sets a device default, and lets each tag override it. Addressing is raw and zero-based, with a switch for the 40001 style, so the numbers in your drawings go straight in.</p>
<pre>holding:100:float32:CDAB    a float across registers 100-101, word-swapped
holding:40:bit3             bit 3 of register 40
input:12:int16              a signed input register
coil:5                      a single coil</pre>
  <p>Adjacent registers merge into one request, so a device with sixty tags is a handful of reads, not sixty.</p>

  <h2>It keeps working when the uplink does not</h2>
  <p>The Edge Relay forwards as a Sparkplug B edge node, with births, sequence numbers, and deaths the way the spec says. When the link drops, readings queue to disk and replay in order, so the historian upstream ends up with the outage backfilled and not a hole. Delivery is at least once.</p>
